{
  "result": "On the last day of the year, the town librarian put a cardboard box beside the returns slot. A sign on it read: THINGS YOU MEANT TO SAY. She expected complaints about the heating. By noon someone had slipped in a recipe for plum cake and a single blue mitten.\n\nThe mitten belonged to a boy who came back for it after lunch. He said he had meant to say goodbye to winter, which he found unnecessarily long. The librarian gave him his mitten and explained that winter had several months left. He considered this, then asked for more paper.\n\nAt closing time, she took the box upstairs. There was a note thanking the bus driver for waiting, another asking whoever played the trumpet on Birch Street to keep practicing, and a third that said, simply, *I knew*. She turned that one over, hoping for a clue. There was none.\n\nShe had planned to go straight home, but the bakery was still open. In the window, beside a tray of buns, stood six purple plums. These were for tomorrow\u2019s tarts, the baker told her. He sold them anyway and asked whether she had ever tried adding black pepper to cake.\n\nLater, while the cake cooled, she called her sister. They had not spoken since August, when an argument about a borrowed ladder had turned into an argument about their mother. Her sister answered. Before she could say hello, the librarian asked, \u201cDo you want to come over for plum cake?\u201d",
